{ The Réti and the Zukertort. White starts with 1.Nf3, keeps the centre flexible and puts the f1 bishop on g2, where it hits the long diagonal. The plan is the same against almost any reply: fianchetto, castle, pressure the centre with c4, and decide on e4 or d4 when you see what Black has done. You play White. }
1. Nf3
{ [%eval 0.24] The Zukertort Opening. White develops a knight and keeps every option open. It is the first move of the Réti, where White controls the centre with pieces and long-range bishops from the flanks, rather than occupying it with pawns at once. }
1... d5 { [%eval 0.24] Black takes the centre at once. } ( 1... Nf6
{ [%eval 0.27] Black develops and controls the centre. } 2. g3
{ [%eval 0.18] White keeps a flexible setup whatever Black does: g3, Bg2, O-O and then d3 or d4. With Black on the same plan it is a King's Indian reversed. }
2... g6 { [%eval 0.26] Black plans the same. } 3. Bg2
{ [%eval 0.25] The bishop takes the long diagonal. } 3... Bg7
{ [%eval 0.24] Black does the same. } 4. O-O { [%eval 0.18] White castles. }
4... O-O { [%eval 0.24] Black castles too. } 5. d3
